<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mont Lassois</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mont Lassois is a hill in the commune of Vix, near Châtillon-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in the Côte-d'Or, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, that rises about 100 m above the upper Seine valley and is known for its remains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a>. At the end of the Hallstatt period it was an oppidum that seems to have controlled the circulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>; its people, ruled in the 6th century BCE by a female aristocracy, taxed the passing convoys. A rich tumulus at Vix holds a woman, probably a queen or a priestess, and excavations since 2002 have revealed the Vix Palace, a very large building. The city of Latiscum later stood on the hill, which is crowned by the church of Saint-Marcel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mont-lassois.jpg" alt="Mont Lassois" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claude PIARD · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mont_Lassois02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mont Lassois has the shape of a J running from south to north. Its main side, known as Mont Saint-Marcel, rises to 306.4 metres, and a second side running east to west, Mont Roussillon, stands at 280 metres, with the church of Saint-Marcel between the two. Coppice and woods cover much of the hill, with grassy clearings on its upper part and cereal and forage crops lower down. Vines, abandoned at the beginning of the 20th century, have been grown on its slopes again since 1980, with pinot noir and chardonnay grapes for crémant du Châtillonnais.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The hill commands the course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> and has been occupied since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt period</a> its role seems to have passed for a time to Vertillum, a site of the Lingones 20 km away. Late in the Gallo-Roman period a vicus on the lower slopes and the plain led to the reoccupation of the hill and the rise of the city of Latiscum. According to the late account of a monk of Pothières, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> of Crocus destroyed Latiscum at the end of the 4th century, but it was quickly rebuilt.</p><p>Around 451 Saint Loup, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, took refuge there from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, and a Merovingian necropolis near the summit and coins bearing the name Latiscum show that it was a major town in the Early Middle Ages. In the Carolingian period the settlement moved to Mont Roussillon, where an oratory was dedicated to Saint Marcel in the 9th century. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-roussillon">Girart de Roussillon</a> built a castle there, but in May 859 a provincial council under Rémy, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, stripped him of his offices and his fortress was razed. A Norman incursion later ruined the site, and occupation of the hill seems to have ceased during the 14th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Romanesque church of Saint-Marcel, whose walls partly reuse sarcophagi from a Merovingian cemetery and whose square bell tower dates from the 15th century, has been classified as a historic monument since 1914. Because the hill stood empty between the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt period</a> and Gallo-Roman times, its Celtic remains are unusually open to excavation. Since 2002 summer campaigns coordinated by Bruno Chaume, with teams from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, have uncovered a rampart around the foot of the hill and a probable farm on the plain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mont Lassois” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a zone from Burgundy to southern Germany lay with rulers based at fortified hilltop seats, such as the Heuneburg on the upper Danube, which in the 6th century BCE was given a wall of mud brick on stone footings in the Mediterranean manner, and <b>Mont Lassois</b> in Burgundy.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The Vix krater, a Greek vessel buried in the rich grave at the foot of <b>Mont Lassois</b> in eastern France, is the largest known metal vessel from Western classical antiquity.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
